// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Safety — Phase 9. FR-35, FR-36, FR-37, FR-71, §23, §42.5.
//
// REPORT is not CHALLENGE. §23.1 is explicit: "A routine challenge means 'this information is
// no longer correct.' A report means 'this may be abusive or unsafe.'" They are stored
// separately because they are answered differently — a challenge is answered by a revision
// from anyone, a report is answered by an administrator, and conflating them would either put
// a moderator in front of ordinary corrections or leave phishing to be fixed by consensus.
//
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// **Reports are not public, and that is a deliberate difference from confirmations and
// challenges.**
//
// A challenge is a claim about information and belongs beside it. A report is an accusation
// about *conduct* — phishing, impersonation, harassment — and a public accusation board would
// be a defamation surface and a brigading target, neither of which serves a student. What the
// public sees is the **outcome**: whether the content is quarantined. Who reported it, how
// many did, and what they wrote stay with the administrator (§23.3).
//
// Classification and visibility are separate questions. `src/domain/models.ts` files Report
// under `communitySignal` because that governs *write* rules — community-authored, never
// deleted by a normal user — while the read layer governs who sees it.
//
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// **No numeric threshold appears in this file, and none appears in the code either.**
//
// §23.2 leaves thresholds as "an operational decision ... not fixed in this concept baseline",
// and CLAUDE.md §11 lists them as open. More decisively, FR-71 and invariant 14 forbid a raw
// count being the *sole automatic determinant* of any state change — so automatic quarantine
// on a report count is not merely unspecified, it is prohibited. Quarantine is therefore an
// explicit administrator action, and the question of "how many reports" never has to be
// answered by guessing.
//
// **There is no attachment column and there must never be one.** Reports are structured and
// textual: a category, free text, and a pointer to the field (§8.6, decided 2026-09-02).
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

/// "This may be dangerous" — FR-35, FR-37, §23.1.
model Report {
  id      String @id @default(cuid())
  fieldId String
  field   Field  @relation(fields: [fieldId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)

  reason ReportReason
  /// What the reporter saw, in their words. Text only — there is nowhere to attach a file.
  detail String?

  /// Null only for a system report. A report from nobody is not evidence of anything.
  reporterId String?
  reporter   User?   @relation("ReportsFiled", fields: [reporterId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)

  createdAt DateTime @default(now())

  // ── Administrator handling (§23.2, §23.3) ──────────────────────────────────
  /// Set when an administrator has decided. Until then the report is open.
  handledAt   DateTime?
  handledById String?
  handledBy   User?          @relation("ReportsHandled", fields: [handledById], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)
  outcome     ReportOutcome?
  /// The administrator's reasoning, kept so a decision can be reviewed later.
  outcomeNote String?

  @@index([fieldId, handledAt])
  @@index([reporterId])
  @@map("reports")
}
